---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/site";
const toc = [{ id: "setup", text: "Set up" }, { id: "tools", text: "Tools" }, { id: "remote", text: "Remote server" }, { id: "llms", text: "llms.txt" }];
const config = `{\n  "mcpServers": {\n    "vinui": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@vinish/ui", "mcp"] }\n  }\n}`;
---
<Docs title="AI and MCP" description="Give Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code and other AI tools the real VinUI code in your style." current="mcp" toc={toc}>
  <div class="crumbs"><a href={href("/")}>VinUI</a><span>AI and MCP</span></div>
  <h1 class="page-title">AI and MCP</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">MCP (Model Context Protocol) lets AI coding tools call outside tools. The VinUI MCP server hands them the current code for your framework and style, so they don't guess.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="setup">Set up</h2>
    <p>Add this to your AI tool's MCP settings (Claude Code: <code>.mcp.json</code>; Cursor: <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>; VS Code: <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> under <code>"servers"</code>). It runs on your computer and reads your project's <code>vin.json</code>.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="mcp.json" lang="json" code={config} />
    <CodeBlock title="Claude Code" lang="bash" code="claude mcp add vinui -- npx -y @vinish/ui mcp" />

    <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="api">
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What the AI can do</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>list_components</code></td><td>See every component and category</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>search_components</code></td><td>Find the right one ("modal" leads to Dialog)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_component</code></td><td>Get the code for your framework, with props, accessibility notes and examples</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>list_styles</code></td><td>See the 8 styles</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get_theme</code></td><td>Read your vin.json</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>add_component</code></td><td>Write the files, like <code>npx @vinish/ui add</code> (your AI tool asks you first)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="remote">Remote server</h2>
    <p>Tools that take a URL instead of a command can use the hosted server. It can read and search components but not write files.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="URL" lang="text" code="https://vinish.dev/ui/mcp" />
    <p>In a JSON config file (Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code and others):</p>
    <CodeBlock title="mcp.json" lang="json" code={`{\n  "mcpServers": {\n    "vinui": { "type": "http", "url": "https://vinish.dev/ui/mcp" }\n  }\n}`} />
    <p>It has <code>list_components</code>, <code>search_components</code>, <code>get_component</code> and <code>list_styles</code>. Your AI tool then adds the files itself, or tells you the <code>npx @vinish/ui add</code> command to run.</p>

    <h2 id="llms">llms.txt</h2>
    <p>Any AI that reads web pages can use <a href={href("/llms.txt")}>/ui/llms.txt</a> (an index) or <a href={href("/llms-full.txt")}>/ui/llms-full.txt</a> (with HTML for each component). They're rebuilt with every release.</p>
  </div>
</Docs>
